@inject ApiMetadata Api
@* Toolbar's Overview, written by hand after Button's: the editor toolbar first, then the regions,
   other controls, an app header, and how it works with JavaScript off. What doesn't fit and actions are
   topics of their own (Docs/Topics/Toolbar*Topic). Accessibility and See also come from the doc comment. *@

<Heading Level="2">Usage</Heading>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Usage</Code>
<TextBlock>A toolbar is a row of controls that stays on one line, such as an editor's actions. What doesn't fit moves into a … menu at the end, where each control becomes a menu item with the same link, form post or <Code>OnClick</Code>. The toolbar works with <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">JavaScript off</Anchor>, so a phone on a slow connection gets the same toolbar.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Topics</Heading>
<OverviewTopics Component="Component" />

<Heading Level="2">An editor toolbar</Heading>
<TextBlock>A toolbar has its own versions of <Anchor Href="docs/components/button">Button</Anchor>, <Anchor Href="docs/components/icon-button">IconButton</Anchor>, <Anchor Href="docs/components/button-group">ButtonGroup</Anchor> and <Anchor Href="docs/components/menu">Menu</Anchor>: <Code>ToolbarButton</Code>, <Code>ToolbarIconButton</Code>, <Code>ToolbarButtonGroup</Code> and <Code>ToolbarMenu</Code>. They accept the same parameters, and they move into the … menu when the toolbar runs out of room; a group moves as one. Narrow the window to see them move.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="toolbar-editor" />
<TextBlock>Its buttons are <Code>Ghost</Code>, <Code>Neutral</Code> and small unless they set their own <Code>Variant</Code>, <Code>Accent</Code> and <Code>Size</Code>, so one solid button stands out. Put any other control in a <Code>ToolbarItem</Code>. Placed directly in the toolbar, it throws.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Start, center and end</Heading>
<TextBlock>A toolbar has three regions: the start, the center and the end. Items sit at the start by default; to put an item in the center or at the end, set its <Code>Position</Code>. For text that stays in the bar, such as a document's name, use <Code>ToolbarText</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="toolbar-regions" />
<TextBlock>The center stays centered, so the start and the end each get half of the room around it. When room runs out, the wider of the two moves its controls into the menu first.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Other controls in a toolbar</Heading>
<TextBlock>A search field, a SegmentedControl or any other control goes in a <Code>ToolbarItem</Code>. Give the item a <Code>Width</Code>, so the toolbar knows how much room it needs. To show something in its place in the … menu, set <Code>MenuContent</Code>; without it, the item stays in the bar.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="toolbar-filters" />
<List Gap="2">
    <ListItem>A <Code>ToolbarMenu</Code> becomes a submenu in the … menu.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>A control in the bar still posts with the form while its item is in the menu. Give the fields in <Code>MenuContent</Code> a name of their own, such as a <Code>MenuRadioGroup</Code>'s <Code>Name</Code>, so the two don't clash, and read that name too.</ListItem>
</List>

<Heading Level="2">In an app header</Heading>
<TextBlock>A toolbar in an app header contains the header's actions, after the brand, and on phones they move into the … menu. Set <Code>Grow</Code> on the toolbar so it fills the rest of the header, and put its items at the end. <Anchor Href="demos/toolbar/app-header">See it run</Anchor>.</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@InHeader</Code>
<TextBlock>The header stays the page's banner landmark. Navigation links belong in a <Anchor Href="docs/components/site-nav">SiteNav</Anchor>, because a toolbar is for controls.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">With JavaScript off</Heading>
<TextBlock>With JavaScript off, the toolbar already shows the right controls in the bar and in the … menu. When IzzyBlazor's script runs, it measures the toolbar and corrects it if needed.</TextBlock>
<List Gap="2">
    <ListItem>Give each <Code>ToolbarItem</Code> a <Code>Width</Code>, so the toolbar can allow for it.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>With a theme that uses another font, the toolbar can be a few pixels off until IzzyBlazor's script measures it.</ListItem>
</List>

<Heading Level="2">Width and item order</Heading>
<List Gap="2">
    <ListItem>A toolbar's width comes from its container. In a row, or in a box that sizes to its content, give the toolbar <Code>Grow</Code> or a width.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>The toolbar renders its items in the order they reach it. An item inside a component of your own, such as an <Code>AuthorizeView</Code>, comes after the other items in its position. So does an item that an <Code>@@if</Code> adds on interactive pages.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>A cascading value written around an item inside the toolbar doesn't reach the item.</ListItem>
</List>

<Heading Level="2">Labels and localization</Heading>
<TextBlock>The … button has a name for screen readers. A toolbar's buttons have <Code>LoadingLabel</Code> and <Code>NewTabLabel</Code>, as on a <Anchor Href="docs/components/button#labels-and-localization">Button</Anchor>. To set them for the whole app, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/text">Text and other languages</Anchor>.</TextBlock>
<Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Sets</th><th>Default</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><Code>MoreLabel</Code></td><td>The … button's name and tooltip</td><td>"More actions"</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</Prose>

@if (OverviewParts.Of(Component, Api).Count > 0)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Parts</Heading>
}
<OverviewParts Component="Component" />

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
    <DocHtml Html="@ApiComponent.Accessibility" Context="@(Context with { Overview = true })" Size="ProseSize.Md" />
}

@KeyboardTable.Section(ApiComponent)

<Heading Level="2">Parameters</Heading>
<ParameterTable ApiComponent="ApiComponent" ApiHref="@Registry.ApiPageHref(Component)" Context="Context" Compact />

<OverviewSeeAlso Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RegistryComponent Component { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiComponent ApiComponent { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiPageContext Context { get; set; } = null!;

    private const string Usage = """
        <Toolbar Label="Editor">
            <ToolbarButtonGroup Label="History">
                <ToolbarIconButton Icon="Lucide.Undo2" Label="Undo" />
                <ToolbarIconButton Icon="Lucide.Redo2" Label="Redo" />
            </ToolbarButtonGroup>
            <ToolbarSeparator />
            <ToolbarButton StartIcon="Lucide.Link" Priority="1">Link</ToolbarButton>
            @* The main action, at the end, never moves into the … menu; Print is only ever there. *@
            <ToolbarButton Position="ToolbarPosition.End" Overflow="ToolbarOverflow.Never" Accent="Accent.Primary" Variant="ButtonVariant.Solid">Publish</ToolbarButton>
            <ToolbarButton StartIcon="Lucide.Printer" Overflow="ToolbarOverflow.Always">Print</ToolbarButton>
        </Toolbar>
        """;

    private const string InHeader = """
        <AppHeader>
            <SidebarToggle />
            <AppBrand Href="/" Icon="Lucide.Mail" Label="Acme Mail" />
            <Toolbar Label="Mail actions" Grow>
                <ToolbarIconButton Icon="Lucide.Search" Label="Search mail" Position="ToolbarPosition.End" />
                <ToolbarIconButton Icon="Lucide.Bell" Label="Notifications" Position="ToolbarPosition.End" />
                <ToolbarButton Position="ToolbarPosition.End" Priority="1" Accent="Accent.Primary" Variant="ButtonVariant.Solid">Compose</ToolbarButton>
            </Toolbar>
        </AppHeader>
        """;
}
